WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.453
Hi, friends. Gani here. This is Peer Pressure Pause Button.

00:00:14.453 --> 00:00:20.907
Sit tall in your chair. Feet flat on the floor.

00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:24.779
Hands resting on your desk. Shoulders soft.

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:28.651
Take one slow breath with me… and let it go.

00:00:28.651 --> 00:00:37.686
Sometimes friends push. Not with their hands — with their words. "Come on." "Everyone's doing it." "Don't be a baby."

00:00:37.686 --> 00:00:48.011
Sometimes the push is a dare. Sometimes it is a secret that comes with "don't tell." Different words — same push.

00:00:48.011 --> 00:00:59.627
And here is the honest part: the push usually comes from friends you like. That is exactly what makes it hard. Wanting to belong is a real feeling, and it is a strong one.

00:00:59.627 --> 00:01:04.790
But you carry something for moments like that. A pause button. Let me show you where it is.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:16.406
The pause button lives right in the middle of the push. When you feel it start, press it: stop. Feet still. One breath. The moment slows down.

00:01:16.406 --> 00:01:28.023
While it is paused, check two things. Your body — is it tight, buzzy, heavy? And your own knowing — the quiet voice that already knows if this feels right.

00:01:28.023 --> 00:01:39.639
Then you can use a sentence that buys you time. "Let me think." Or a sentence that is the whole answer: "Not for me." Both are complete. Neither one is rude.

00:01:39.639 --> 00:01:49.964
And here is what a pause never costs you: a real friendship. Pressing pause is not an insult. A friend might be disappointed for a minute — and a minute passes.

00:01:49.964 --> 00:01:51.255
If someone needs you to feel bad to keep the moment going, then in that moment, that is not how a friend acts. The moment is the problem — never the person.

00:01:51.255 --> 00:02:02.871
Most pushes end with the pause. But some pushes come with something extra: something unsafe, or something unkind.

00:02:02.871 --> 00:02:14.487
When that happens, a grown-up hears about it. That is the rule, and it is a strong one.

00:02:14.487 --> 00:02:23.522
Telling to get someone in trouble is tattling. Telling to keep someone safe is reporting. Reporting is not a small thing done by small people. Reporting is strength.

00:02:23.522 --> 00:02:24.813
You never have to carry an unsafe secret. Not for a dare, not for a friend, not for anyone.

00:02:24.813 --> 00:02:33.848
Feel the push. Press pause. Trust your knowing.

00:02:33.848 --> 00:02:40.301
Shoulders soft. Let your body rest.

00:02:40.301 --> 00:02:45.464
Hands down. One more slow breath… and let it go.

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:51.917
Eyes open. Look around your classroom.

00:02:51.917 --> 00:02:58.371
You're ready for what's next.
